    {
      "title": "A salt that loses water",
      "stem": "A 4.990 g sample of CuSO₄·xH₂O gives 3.192 g of dry CuSO₄ after repeated heating to constant mass. Use M(CuSO₄)=159.6 and M(H₂O)=18.02 g mol⁻¹.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": "a",
          "prompt": "Determine x, showing the mole ratio.",
          "marks": 3
        },
        {
          "label": "b",
          "prompt": "Describe how to establish constant mass experimentally, including a precaution specific to this residue.",
          "marks": 2
        },
        {
          "label": "c",
          "prompt": "All the dry residue is dissolved and made to 250.0 cm³. Find the Cu²⁺ concentration and the number of sulfate ions in 10.00 cm³ of this solution. Use NA=6.02×10²³ mol⁻¹.",
          "marks": 3
        },
        {
          "label": "d",
          "prompt": "Explain how splattering a little solid out of the crucible during heating would bias x if it were unnoticed.",
          "marks": 2
        }
      ],
      "markScheme": [
        {
          "part": "a",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Mass water=1.798 g, corresponding to 1.798/18.02=0.09978 mol."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Dry salt amount=3.192/159.6=0.02000 mol."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Ratio H₂O:CuSO₄=4.99:1, so x=5."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Heat, cool in a desiccator and weigh, then repeat heating/cooling/weighing."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Stop when consecutive cooled masses agree within the balance precision; protection from moist air limits rehydration."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "c",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "[Cu²⁺]=0.02000/0.2500=0.08000 mol dm⁻³."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "n(SO₄²⁻) in 10.00 cm³=0.08000×0.01000=8.000×10⁻⁴ mol."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Number=4.82×10²⁰ sulfate ions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "d",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "The lost solid would be assigned to water loss, overestimating water amount."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "The measured dry-salt amount is too small, so the ratio and calculated x are too large."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "marks": 10,
      "questionType": "structured",
      "answerSummary": "The measured dry-salt amount is too small, so the ratio and calculated x are too large.",
