    {
      "title": "Selecting a conductor",
      "stem": "An engineering sample consists of copper containing 70.0% isotope 63 and 30.0% isotope 65 by number. For this question take their relative isotopic masses as exactly 63 and 65. Copper has atomic number 29.",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": "a",
          "prompt": "Calculate the relative atomic mass. State the numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons in ⁶⁵Cu²⁺.",
          "marks": 3
        },
        {
          "label": "b",
          "prompt": "Write noble-gas configurations for neutral Cu and Cu²⁺.",
          "marks": 2
        },
        {
          "label": "c",
          "prompt": "Explain why a copper wire conducts and can be drawn into a thinner wire without losing its metallic bonding.",
          "marks": 3
        },
        {
          "label": "d",
          "prompt": "The supplier claims that isotope enrichment will greatly strengthen the wire by disrupting the lattice in the same way as adding a different metal. Evaluate the claim.",
          "marks": 2
        }
      ],
      "markScheme": [
        {
          "part": "a",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Ar=0.700×63+0.300×65=63.60."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "29 protons and 36 neutrons."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "27 electrons."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "b",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Cu: [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Cu²⁺: [Ar]3d⁹; the 4s electron is removed before a 3d electron."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "c",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Positive metal ion cores are attracted to delocalized electrons."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Mobile electrons carry electrical charge."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Layers of cores can slip while non-directional attraction to the electrons persists."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "part": "d",
          "steps": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Isotopes have the same nuclear charge and essentially the same electron structure and atomic size."
            },
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "text": "Unlike different-sized alloy atoms, isotope substitution does not cause comparable size distortion; the proposed mechanism does not justify the claim."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "marks": 10,
      "questionType": "structured",
      "answerSummary": "Unlike different-sized alloy atoms, isotope substitution does not cause comparable size distortion; the proposed mechanism does not justify the claim.",
